WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.609
Let's go next to our caller from the five,
seven zero area code.

00:00:04.609 --> 00:00:06.930
Who's calling today from five, seven zero.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.370
Hey David, does that mean

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:10.839
yes, it is.

00:00:10.839 --> 00:00:12.790
Hey, what's going on?

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:14.249
This is Nick.

00:00:14.249 --> 00:00:15.249
Yes.

00:00:15.249 --> 00:00:20.920
I watched your video either was yesterday
or a couple of days ago about, um, Bernie

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.140
Sanders a new proposal.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.830
Um, in terms of campaign finance reform right
now, obviously I'm super for this.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:33.410
Um, I'm pretty sure, um, large majority, I
think they pulled at one time about 90% of

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:39.440
Americans are for, um, some sort of campaign
finance reform and uh, just overall finance

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.640
reform in general for, um, uh, politics.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.390
So, uh, it always interests me that this,
these things never get passed.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:50.960
I think burns' proposals depends really super
effective if it, if it's implemented even

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:52.000
some parts of it.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.290
Um, I wanted to ask you, what do you think
some of the, uh, um, issues at hand, uh, it

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:02.300
being not being able to pass with such a,
you know, large majority in public opinion.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:07.420
Um, especially, you know, you talk about things
like gun control, um, having such a large

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.200
majority about things like using universal
background checks, the wanting to be passed.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.729
But at the end of the day, I think this might
even be more important in lead to other changes.

00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:17.520
So what do you think are some of the big obstacles
standing in the way of it?

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.970
Well, the biggest obstacle is that it's hard
to get people who are in power because of

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:22.970
the system.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.939
We have to change the system that that's like
fundamentally obstacles.

00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:29.020
So as you're pointing out, I don't know the
pole you're talking about, but it wouldn't

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:33.280
surprise me that 90% of the country wants
at least some kind of a of campaign finance

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:34.390
reform.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:38.369
Those aren't the people who are in positions
of power because of the system, right?

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:43.939
Someone who works in the local bricklayers
union or whatever that wants campaign finance

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:49.929
reform, they didn't get their job the way
that a Senator did because of the system of

00:01:49.929 --> 00:01:52.130
campaign finance that we have.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:54.850
And so Senator is not going to want to change
that system.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:59.270
So number one is we need to start electing
at every level of government people who are

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.090
committed to doing campaign finance reform.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:03.750
But there's also logistical obstacles.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:09.330
So like for example, one of the tenants, one
of the ideas of Bernie's campaign finance

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:14.330
reform money out of politics plan is you start
doing public financing of elections.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:19.440
You are not going to be able to even make
any headway on that for as long as the prevailing

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.360
law of the land is that money is speech, right?

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:29.170
So then you now have to link that provision
to can we overturn or somehow undo citizens

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:30.170
United.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.720
And so the, the, the real obstacle is that
a lot of, you know, Bernie laid out enough

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:38.140
different proposals that some could be handled
legislatively, some could maybe be done by

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.700
executive order, but there is stuff in there
that you, you know, listen for as long as

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.620
money is speech, it's going to be very difficult
to do a lot of those things.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.730
And so there are, the obstacles are systemic
and they are big, but they are worth tackling

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:54.130
for sure as it sounds like you agree with.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:55.920
Yeah, that's true.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.010
I totally agree with that point.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:01.060
Um, and of course the citizens United case,
I feel like I agree with Tanya that has to

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.880
be, um, overturned for any headway to be made
on there.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:08.370
Um, additionally, on top of that, do you think,
um, you know, Bernie is elected, a lot of

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:11.520
those initiatives will get pushed initially
through executive order.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.390
Do you think there'll be maybe some bipartisan
support, um, in the house and Senate?

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:17.500
You know, I don't know the answer to that.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:21.000
I, I would, I would really have to look at
the list because not everything is even potentially

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.320
doable by executive order.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.500
So I would need to really look at the list
first to even see what is conceivably doable

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:27.570
by executive order.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.310
I don't have the answer for you right now.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:30.310
All right, great.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.540
Yeah, thanks for taking my call then.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:33.540
All right.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:34.540
I appreciate it.

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:35.540
Thank you so much.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:36.540
Hope to hear from you again.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:37.560
That was a Nick.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:38.580
Great to hear from Nick.

